<worksheet>
  <title>Hotel Revenue</title>

  <!-- Optional introduction -->
  <objectives>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <p>
                Use quadratic functions to model revenue.
            </p>
        </li>
        
    </ul>
  </objectives>
<introduction>
    <p>
       You are in charge of setting the room rental rate at the hotel you manage. This responsibility has implications because if you charge a low daily rate for each room then lots of rooms will be rented, but on the other hand, if you charge a really high daily rate then only a few rooms will be rented.  Suppose you know the following:
    </p>

     
<ul>
    <li>    
        <p>
        The hotel has 210 rooms.
        </p>
    </li>
    <li>
        <p>
           If you set the daily room rate to $50, then all 210 rooms will be rented. 
        </p>
    </li>
<li>
    <p>
      For each $1 increase in the daily room rate, three fewer rooms are rented. 
    </p>
</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
        Begin by exploring the relationship between the price of a room and the number of rooms that are rented.  
    </p>
  </introduction>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
        <p>
           If you set the room rate to $51, how many rooms are rented? How about if the room rate is $52? Or $65? To help find a pattern, fill in the following table:
        </p>

        <table>
            <tabular top="major" bottom="major" left="major" right="major" row="minor" column="minor">
                <row>
                    <cell>Room Rate ($)</cell>
                    <cell>Number of Rooms Rented, N</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>50</cell>
                    <cell>210</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>51</cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>52</cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>65</cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>75</cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                </row>
                 <row>
                    <cell>85</cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                </row>
                 <row>
                    <cell>95</cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                </row>
                 <row>
                    <cell>100</cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><m>x</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>N(x)=</m></cell></row>
            </tabular>
        </table>
    </statement>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
        <p>
           What type of function is <m>N(x)</m>?
        </p>
    </statement>

  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Now let's explore the revenue from renting rooms.  Recall that revenue is the amount of money that a business collects from selling items.  For example, if my candy company is selling candy bars for $1.75 each and I sell 7 candy bars then my revenue is 7*1.75= $12.25.
        </p>

        <p>
          Let's apply this idea to renting rooms.   
        </p>
    </statement>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
               Suppose you set the room rate to $50. Use the table above to determine how many rooms would be rented (i.e. sold). What is your revenue at this room rate? 
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
               Suppose you set the room rate to $65. Use the table above to determine how many rooms would be rented (i.e. sold). What is your revenue at this room rate?
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
               Suppose you set the room rate to $100. Determine how many rooms would be rented. What is your revenue at this room rate?
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
<task>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Summarize this in the following table and look for a pattern:
        </p>
        <table>
            <tabular top="major" bottom="major" left="major" right="major" row="minor" column="minor">
                <row>
                    <cell>Room Rate ($)</cell>
                    <cell>Number of Rooms Rented, <m>N(x)</m></cell>
                <cell>Revenue, <m>R(x)\quad\quad\quad\quad\quad\quad\quad\quad</m></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>50</cell>
                    <cell>210</cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>51</cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                    <cell></cell>   
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>52</cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>65</cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                    <cell></cell>  
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>75</cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                </row>
                 <row>
                    <cell>85</cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                </row>
                 <row>
                    <cell>95</cell>
                    <cell></cell><cell></cell>  
                </row>
                 <row>
                    <cell>100</cell>
                    <cell></cell><cell></cell>  
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><m>x</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>N(x)=</m></cell><cell><m>R(x)=</m></cell>
                </row>
            </tabular>
        </table>
    </statement>
    
</task>

  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <introduction>
        <p>
           You want to maximize your revenue.  
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                What room rate would you set to maximize your revenue? 
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                How many rooms would be rented at this room rate? 
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                What is your maximum revenue?
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task> 
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                How do you know that this is the maximum revenue?
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>
  </worksheet>